        <p>Year one belongs to the bonus: it keeps $3,517.50, which is $674.14 more than the raise keeps in its first year. Year two belongs to the raise: the bonus pays nothing twice, while the raise keeps another $2,843.36, so two years together are $5,686.72 for the raise against $3,517.50 for the bonus, a gap of $2,169.22 the other way. One honesty note on the bonus figure: the $1,100.00 federal line is withholding at the flat 22% supplemental rate our <a href="/calculators/bonus-tax/">bonus-tax calculator</a> uses. At your 24% marginal rate the final federal tax on the bonus is $1,200.00, so the true year-end keep is $3,417.50, not $3,517.50, which makes the bonus win year one by $574.14 instead of $674.14. Either way the story holds: a bonus is a one-year sugar hit, a raise compounds, and the raise also lifts every overtime hour, every paid day off, and every future raise that is a percentage of the new rate. Take the bonus if you need cash this year. Take the raise if you plan to stay.</p>
