        <p>A 45-minute commute each way is 90 minutes a day, or 390 hours a year, which is 9.75 forty-hour weeks of unpaid time, worth $18,907.20 of kept pay. Put another way, it turns a 40-hour week into a 47.5-hour week for the same money, because 390 hours divided by 52 weeks is 7.5 extra hours a week. The comparison that stings most is the bonus in the section above: the whole $5,000 bonus keeps $3,517.50, while the 45-minute commute costs $18,907.20, more than five times as much, every single year. Cutting the commute from 45 minutes to 15 minutes each way saves 260 hours a year, worth $12,604.80 of kept pay, which is more than a $2-an-hour raise keeps in a year. A shorter commute is not a lifestyle perk at this rate. It is worth more than most raises, and it is the one raise you can give yourself without asking anyone.</p>
