        <p>One honest note before you go further: that take-home figure is federal only, for a single filer taking the $16,100 standard deduction, with no state tax and no 401(k) or health premium taken off. It also assumes 40 paid hours in all 52 weeks, which no real year quite delivers. The $2,217.60 in Step 2 is the detail most guides skip: $9,240 of the $114,940 taxable income actually sits in the 24% bracket, about 29% bigger than the $7,160 slice at $62, and the flat-22% shortcut would overstate your federal tax by $5,103.20. The bracket-slice section below prices exactly what that means for your next raise. The sections that follow run the same $63.00 rate through every schedule, overtime, and unpaid-week variation, so you can read the number that matches your real year.</p>
