        <div class="methodology-box">
          <h3>Sources &amp; Method</h3>
          <p>Gross figures use the standard full-time year of 2,080 hours (40 hours × 52 weeks). Monthly averages divide the annual total by 12, and biweekly figures use 26 paychecks. The after-tax sketch uses 2026 federal rules for a single filer taking the standard deduction: standard deduction $16,100, brackets 10% to $12,400, 12% to $50,400, 22% to $105,700 of taxable income, 24% to $201,775, per IRS Revenue Procedure 2025-32. FICA is 7.65% (Social Security 6.2% to the $184,500 wage base, Medicare 1.45%), with the income below the wage base. Median wage: Bureau of Labor Statistics, Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics, May 2025 ($24.51 an hour); 75th-percentile wage $38.71 an hour (BLS, May 2025); median weekly earnings for full-time workers $1,251 in the second quarter of 2026 (BLS). Every take-home figure on this page comes from the same method, so the tables agree with each other to the cent. Competitor pages for this rate show the same gaps: takehomepay.online gives a federal figure of $20,988.10, $804.50 too high, and its headline $92,164.94 is a California figure; SuperMoney gives a rough $98,557 take-home, $2,274.84 low, on a page still quoting 2021 BLS pay data; applyquick.ai uses 2025 brackets and lands at $100,499 for a no-state-tax worker, $332.84 low; timehackhero uses 2023 brackets and a flat 24% and lands at $88,867.15, $11,964.69 low; paycheckscalc.com answers a U.S. query with Australian figures, a 38-hour week, $124,488 gross, and $93,864 take-home after Australian tax; personalfinanceblogs uses a flat 20% and lands at $104,832, and quotes $10,080 for both the monthly and the biweekly figure, when $10,080 is the four-week month and the real biweekly gross is $5,040; calculateme uses a 50-week year and lands at $126,000 gross; PayMath matches our $100,831.84 take-home, but its federal figure of $20,184 is $0.40 above our $20,183.60, and it shows no bracket steps, no unpaid weeks, no worked overtime example, and no raise or benefits comparison, and its 23.1% “total tax rate” mixes FICA into one rate. This article is general information, not tax or legal advice.</p>
        </div>
